# FACADE DESIGN PATTERN

=========================================================

## 1. DEFINITION

=========================================================

The **Facade Pattern** is a **Structural Design Pattern** that provides a **single, simplified interface** to a complex subsystem.

It hides internal complexity and exposes **one entry point** for the client.

**One-Line Definition**

> Facade provides a unified interface to a set of subsystems.

---

## 2. WHY DO WE NEED FACADE?

Without Facade:

* Client communicates with multiple subsystem classes.
* Client knows execution order.
* High coupling.
* Difficult to maintain.

With Facade:

* Client calls only one method.
* Facade coordinates all subsystem interactions.

---

## 3. INTENT

✔ Simplify subsystem usage

✔ Hide implementation complexity

✔ Reduce client-subsystem coupling

✔ Provide a single entry point

---

## 4. STRUCTURE

```text
            Client
               │
               ▼
            Facade
       ┌──────┼──────┐
       ▼      ▼      ▼
   Payment Inventory Email
```

---

## 5. COMPONENTS

* **Client** → Uses only the Facade.
* **Facade** → Coordinates subsystem calls.
* **Subsystems** → Perform actual business logic.

---

## 6. RELATIONSHIP

Uses **Composition (HAS-A)**

```text
OrderFacade HAS-A PaymentGateway

OrderFacade HAS-A Inventory

OrderFacade HAS-A InvoiceSystem

OrderFacade HAS-A EmailService
```

Facade does **NOT** modify subsystem behavior.

---

## 7. BENEFITS

✔ Simplifies client code

✔ Reduces coupling

✔ Hides complexity

✔ Easier maintenance

✔ Centralized workflow

---

## 8. DRAWBACKS

❌ Can become a God Class

❌ Extra abstraction layer

❌ May hide advanced subsystem functionality

---

## 9. REAL-WORLD EXAMPLES

* Hotel Reception
* Order Processing
* Banking App
* Deployment Pipeline
* Computer Startup

---

## 10. WITHOUT FACADE

Client manages everything.

```text
Client
   │
   ├── Payment
   ├── Inventory
   ├── Invoice
   └── Email
```

### Code

```cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class PaymentGateway
{
public:
    void processPayment()
    {
        cout << "Payment processed." << endl;
    }
};

class Inventory
{
