## 12. MOST ASKED INTERVIEW QUESTIONS

**Q. What problem does Facade solve?**

Provides a single entry point to multiple subsystems and reduces client complexity.

---

**Q. Why not let the client call subsystem classes directly?**

That increases coupling and forces the client to know the subsystem workflow.

---

**Q. Does Facade change subsystem behavior?**

No. It only coordinates subsystem calls.

---

**Q. Does Facade use inheritance?****************************

No. It mainly uses **Composition (HAS-A)**.

---

**Q. Can subsystem classes be used without the Facade?**

Yes. Facade is optional.

---

**Q. Which SOLID principle does it support?**

* SRP (Client doesn't coordinate subsystems.)
* DIP (Client depends on Facade instead of many subsystem classes.)

---

## 13. FACADE vs ADAPTER

| Facade                 | Adapter                           |
| ---------------------- | --------------------------------- |
| Simplifies a subsystem | Converts one interface to another |
| Single entry point     | Compatibility wrapper             |
| Hides complexity       | Changes interface                 |

---

## 14. FACADE vs DECORATOR

| Facade                     | Decorator                      |
| -------------------------- | ------------------------------ |
| Simplifies subsystem usage | Adds new behavior              |
| One entry point            | Wraps an object                |
| Uses composition           | Uses inheritance + composition |

---

## 15. INTERVIEW ONE-LINER

> **Facade provides a single, simplified interface to a complex subsystem, 
hiding internal complexity and reducing coupling between the client and subsystem classes.**

